  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q15-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Find the values of $c$ for which the graph of $y=\\sin x$ is tangent to the graph of $y=\\cos x+c$ at exactly one point in the interval $[0,2\\pi]$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$c=\\pm\\sqrt2$.\n\nThese are the maximum and minimum values of $\\sin x-\\cos x$.",
      "worked": "At an intersection,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin x&=\\cos x+c\\\\\\sin x-\\cos x&=c.\\end{align*}$$\nOn the unit circle, write $X=\\cos x$ and $Y=\\sin x$. The equation $Y-X=c$ represents parallel lines of gradient $1$. A line touches the unit circle once when its perpendicular distance from the centre equals the radius:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{|c|}{\\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2}}&=1\\\\|c|&=\\sqrt2.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the difference $\\sin x-\\cos x$ has maximum $\\sqrt2$ at $x=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$ and minimum $-\\sqrt2$ at $x=\\frac{7\\pi}{4}$. Each occurs once in $[0,2\\pi]$. At either extremum the two graphs touch without crossing, so\n$$c=\\pm\\sqrt2.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
